{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 25, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a powerful ruler of the County of Tripoli who reigned from 1183 to 1187. He is a significant figure in the Crusades particularly known for his military leadership and his role in defending the territory of Tripoli against Saladin's forces. His reign marked a period of considerable expansion and consolidation of Tripoli's power.\n\n*Early Life and Ascension*\n\nRaymond III was the son of Raymond IV Count of Tripoli and his wife Gertrude. He inherited the county upon his father's death in 1183. His early reign was characterized by internal conflicts and challenges to his authority. He faced opposition from various factions within the county and struggled to maintain control over the region's diverse population including both Christians and Muslims. He navigated these internal issues skillfully solidifying his position through a combination of diplomacy and military strength.\n\nRaymond III's reign saw Tripoli expand its territories notably gaining control over the coastal region of Syria which was previously under the control of the Seljuk Turks. He established a strong military force and implemented administrative reforms to improve governance. He fostered trade and economic prosperity making Tripoli a significant center of commerce in the Levant.\n\n*Military Campaigns and Conflicts*\n\nRaymond III's most famous achievement was his successful defense of Tripoli during the Third Crusade. He played a crucial role in the siege of Acre a key Crusader stronghold. Despite being outnumbered and facing formidable adversaries including Saladin's armies Raymond III skillfully defended the city and prevented its capture. This victory significantly boosted the morale of the Crusader forces and helped to preserve the Crusader presence in the Levant.\n\nHe also engaged in numerous other military campaigns against Muslim forces in the region often battling for control of territory and resources. His military prowess earned him respect and admiration throughout the Crusader states and among the local populations. He was known for his strategic thinking and his ability to adapt to changing circumstances in battle.\n\n*Legacy and Decline*\n\nRaymond III died in 1187 during the Siege of Acre. His death marked the end of his reign and the beginning of a period of instability in Tripoli. Although he successfully defended the county during the Third Crusade his legacy is complex. He is remembered as a valiant defender of the Crusader states but also as a ruler who faced numerous challenges and internal conflicts.\n\nHis reign laid the foundation for the future development of Tripoli as a significant power in the Levant. While the county eventually declined in importance following his death it remained a vital trading center and a strategically important location for centuries. Raymond III's military achievements and his role in the Crusades solidified his place in history as one of the most prominent rulers of the County of Tripoli. He is often portrayed as a figure of courage and resilience in the face of adversity.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.1805190145969391}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": -1.6689286894688848e-06}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -4.8874615458771586e-05}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -2.622600959512056e-06}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁was", "logprob": -0.07509470731019974}, {"token": "▁a", "logprob": -0.02148974873125553}, {"token": "▁powerful", "logprob": -0.5968805551528931}, {"token": "▁ruler", "logprob": -0.5940437912940979}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -0.9444845914840698}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.00048792376765049994}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.04363187775015831}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -2.4318398573086597e-05}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "▁who", "logprob": -2.7839522